# Other systems this operator may reach

## What to write here

Anything {{ company_prog_name }} can call that is not the mail engine and is not
already in `CLAUDE.md`: an internal API, an admin console, a back-office that
the {{ email_address }} identity happens to be authorised for. `CLAUDE.md`
points here from its engine section, so whatever you write becomes part of the
operator manual an agent reads at the start of every session.

For each system, four things — and if you cannot give all four, leave it out
rather than half-document it:

- **What it is**, in one sentence, and what it is good for.
- **How the operator authenticates.** If it rides on an existing session
  (the same Firebase identity, a shared token), say so; never paste a credential
  here — secrets live in `~/.{{ company_slug }}-cs/.env`.
- **The exact base URL of each realm**, and which one is production.
- **Which calls only read, and which mutate.** Mark every mutating call as
  needing the operator's explicit go, per action.

Put the date you last checked each claim next to it, in `YYYY-MM-DD` form, and
say what you actually called to check it.

## Why it matters

An agent reads this file as a description of the present. A base URL that moved,
an endpoint that used to be read-only, a permission that was revoked — none of
that announces itself, and an undated "verified, works" is indistinguishable
from a claim somebody guessed. The dates are what let the next session tell a
fact from a fossil.

If nothing outside the engine is reachable from this clone, say exactly that in
one line and delete the rest. An empty file and "there is nothing else" read very
differently to whoever comes next.
